1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,731
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,751 --> 00:00:08,639
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,039 --> 00:00:15,139
This is Lecture 10, coverstitch and chainstitch machines.

4
00:00:15,539 --> 00:00:20,593
Turn a T-shirt over and look at the hem. Two straight lines on the face,

5
00:00:20,613 --> 00:00:23,694
and a net covering the raw edge on the back.

6
00:00:23,714 --> 00:00:28,486
The back rise of a pair of jeans has two strong rows of chainstitch.

7
00:00:28,506 --> 00:00:33,138
Neither uses a rotary hook or a bobbin. A looper forms the stitch,

8
00:00:33,158 --> 00:00:37,930
and the thread comes straight off large cones. Today, two questions.

9
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:40,961
Which two moments make or break stitch 401,

10
00:00:40,981 --> 00:00:44,696
and how many cones does a batch of T-shirt hems need?

11
00:00:46,216 --> 00:00:47,956
The shop-floor problem.

12
00:00:48,356 --> 00:00:52,704
A knitwear factory has an order for 1000 T-shirts:

13
00:00:52,724 --> 00:00:58,819
about 100 centimetres of hem and 72 of cuffs, 172 centimetres a shirt,

14
00:00:58,839 --> 00:01:05,283
sewn in 406 two-needle three-thread on a cylinder-bed coverstitch machine,

15
00:01:05,303 --> 00:01:11,747
at 7 stitches per centimetre, fabric 0.5 millimetres, gauge 4 millimetres,

16
00:01:11,767 --> 00:01:15,329
wastage 12 percent, 5000 metres per cone.

17
00:01:15,729 --> 00:01:20,519
The store supervisor takes the published ratio of 18,

18
00:01:20,539 --> 00:01:25,602
works out a total length and plans to divide it by 5000.

19
00:01:26,002 --> 00:01:30,606
How much does each thread need, how many cones of each,

20
00:01:30,626 --> 00:01:33,381
and what's wrong with his method?

21
00:01:34,901 --> 00:01:38,481
The chainstitch family.

22
00:01:40,001 --> 00:01:45,222
What chainstitch gains: thread comes from large cones, with no bobbin,

23
00:01:45,242 --> 00:01:49,115
so long seams go in one pass; the loops can open up,

24
00:01:49,135 --> 00:01:52,634
so the seam stretches a lot, which suits knits;

25
00:01:52,654 --> 00:01:57,501
and a coverstitch seams and covers the raw edge in one operation.

26
00:01:57,901 --> 00:02:02,834
What it loses: it uses much more thread, its end can unravel,

27
00:02:02,854 --> 00:02:06,001
and it can't be locked by back-tacking.

28
00:02:06,401 --> 00:02:18,932
On the right, Virtual Lab 10-2 cycles through 401, 406, 407, 602 and 605,

29
00:02:18,952 --> 00:02:21,339
face and back.

30
00:02:21,739 --> 00:02:29,026
406 and 407 have two or three needles sharing one looper;

31
00:02:29,046 --> 00:02:33,000
602 and 605 add a cover thread.

32
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:37,278
How stitch 401 forms.

33
00:02:38,798 --> 00:02:43,460
One 401 stitch has four moments.

34
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:46,974
One, the catch at about 214 degrees:

35
00:02:46,994 --> 00:02:53,415
the needle has risen about 3.4 millimetres and the looper point enters the

36
00:02:53,435 --> 00:02:55,853
loop from behind the needle.

37
00:02:56,253 --> 00:03:00,602
Two, the needle leaves, the fabric feeds,

38
00:03:00,622 --> 00:03:08,593
and the looper reaches its leftmost point at about 356, spreading the loop.

39
00:03:08,993 --> 00:03:13,743
Three, at about 130 degrees the looper is on its way back,

40
00:03:13,763 --> 00:03:15,634
in front of the needle,

41
00:03:15,654 --> 00:03:20,568
and the next needle enters the triangle formed by the blade,

42
00:03:20,588 --> 00:03:23,693
the looper thread and the needle loop.

43
00:03:24,093 --> 00:03:24,497
Four,

44
00:03:24,517 --> 00:03:31,618
at about 176 the looper is rightmost and the old loop casts off and is pulled tight.

45
00:03:32,018 --> 00:03:34,798
Success depends on moments one and three.

46
00:03:36,318 --> 00:03:42,018
This is the front-view animation from Virtual Lab 10-1.

47
00:03:42,418 --> 00:03:47,742
The looper is drawn light when it's behind the needle and dark when it's in front.

48
00:03:48,142 --> 00:03:49,598
Watch two moments.

49
00:03:49,998 --> 00:03:55,578
At the catch, the point must be behind the needle, going right into the loop.

50
00:03:55,978 --> 00:03:59,418
When the next needle comes down to looper height,

51
00:03:59,438 --> 00:04:04,361
the point must already be in front of the needle, but not yet past it,

52
00:04:04,381 --> 00:04:07,469
so the needle can enter the thread triangle.

53
00:04:07,869 --> 00:04:14,889
One looper motion decides both, so change one and the other changes too.

54
00:04:16,409 --> 00:04:25,048
Here's the point's path seen from above, with front–back magnified four times.

55
00:04:25,448 --> 00:04:30,575
The left–right swing decides when the point reaches the needle;

56
00:04:30,595 --> 00:04:35,884
the front–back needle-avoiding move decides which side it passes.

57
00:04:36,284 --> 00:04:40,695
Both are roughly harmonic and about 90 degrees apart,

58
00:04:40,715 --> 00:04:46,715
so together they trace a flat ellipse: behind the needle on the way out,

59
00:04:46,735 --> 00:04:48,805
in front on the way back.

60
00:04:49,205 --> 00:04:54,534
At 130 degrees, when the next needle reaches looper height,

61
00:04:54,554 --> 00:05:01,515
the point is 1.4 millimetres left of the needle's centre and 0.41 in front of

62
00:05:01,535 --> 00:05:02,603
its surface.

63
00:05:04,123 --> 00:05:08,079
Write the conditions for a good stitch as five criteria.

64
00:05:08,479 --> 00:05:08,820
One:

65
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:15,942
the needle rise at the catch must be in the range where the loop is big enough,

66
00:05:15,962 --> 00:05:19,819
about 1.0 to 4.2 millimetres in this model.

67
00:05:20,219 --> 00:05:22,053
Two: at the catch,

68
00:05:22,073 --> 00:05:29,984
the point must be at least 0.2 millimetres above the eye and below the top of

69
00:05:30,004 --> 00:05:30,911
the loop;

70
00:05:30,931 --> 00:05:32,559
standard is 1.5.

71
00:05:32,959 --> 00:05:39,891
Three: the clearance on the outward stroke is 0 to 0.1 millimetres;

72
00:05:39,911 --> 00:05:43,419
negative means hitting the needle.

73
00:05:43,819 --> 00:05:48,610
Four: when the needle reaches looper height,

74
00:05:48,630 --> 00:05:54,624
the point is at least 0.3 to its left and 0.2 in front.

75
00:05:55,024 --> 00:06:00,924
Five: no collision while the needle is below looper height.

76
00:06:02,444 --> 00:06:05,132
Move only the looper timing,

77
00:06:05,152 --> 00:06:11,610
and the usable range is about minus 3.5 to plus 4 degrees, under 8.

78
00:06:12,010 --> 00:06:13,870
The two ends fail differently.

79
00:06:14,270 --> 00:06:20,578
Advance it and the point comes back too early: criterion four fails first,

80
00:06:20,598 --> 00:06:25,453
the needle misses the triangle and the chain falls apart.

81
00:06:25,853 --> 00:06:32,989
Retard it and the loop has already shrunk: criterion one fails first,

82
00:06:33,009 --> 00:06:34,753
a skipped stitch.

83
00:06:35,153 --> 00:06:44,124
The looper can be raised about 1.7 millimetres but lowered no more than 0.8;

84
00:06:44,144 --> 00:06:53,115
the retraction must be about 2.45 to 3.8; the avoidance at least about 0.86.

85
00:06:53,515 --> 00:06:57,733
These numbers show how the settings constrain each other;

86
00:06:57,753 --> 00:07:00,037
they're not an adjustment spec.

87
00:07:01,557 --> 00:07:06,381
The coverstitch machine: needles, a looper, a spreader.

88
00:07:07,901 --> 00:07:15,545
The coverstitch stitch zone has a row of needles, one looper and a spreader.

89
00:07:15,945 --> 00:07:23,356
The gauge is 3.2 to 6.4 millimetres, the speed 6500 stitches a minute,

90
00:07:23,376 --> 00:07:27,285
the needle-bar stroke 31 millimetres.

91
00:07:27,685 --> 00:07:33,903
Below the fabric, the looper takes the right, middle and left needles in turn,

92
00:07:33,923 --> 00:07:38,142
so the needles are set at slightly different heights.

93
00:07:38,542 --> 00:07:39,970
Above the fabric,

94
00:07:39,990 --> 00:07:46,362
the spreader is leftmost when the needle bar has come down 1.1 millimetres,

95
00:07:46,382 --> 00:07:52,584
about 25 degrees, laying the cover thread across in front of the needles;

96
00:07:52,604 --> 00:07:57,698
the needle points reach it at about 77 degrees and catch it.

97
00:07:58,098 --> 00:08:02,478
Coverstitch machines have differential feed too.

98
00:08:03,998 --> 00:08:07,058
Thread consumption: how much for a batch.

99
00:08:08,578 --> 00:08:12,433
The geometric model counts thread by thread.

100
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:19,911
Each needle thread uses one stitch length, plus twice the fabric thickness,

101
00:08:19,931 --> 00:08:23,413
plus about 1 millimetre for the loop.

102
00:08:23,813 --> 00:08:29,859
The looper thread uses the number of needles times twice the stitch length

103
00:08:29,879 --> 00:08:31,335
plus 1 millimetre,

104
00:08:31,355 --> 00:08:34,696
plus a stitch length and twice the gauge.

105
00:08:35,096 --> 00:08:39,665
Divide by the stitch length and you get the consumption ratio:

106
00:08:39,685 --> 00:08:42,996
centimetres of thread per centimetre of seam.

107
00:08:43,396 --> 00:08:51,732
At 7 stitches per centimetre, a stitch length of about 1.43 millimetres,

108
00:08:51,752 --> 00:09:00,320
406 gives 2.4 for each needle thread and 12.0 for the looper, 16.8 in all,

109
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:03,222
against the published 18.

110
00:09:03,622 --> 00:09:08,158
The denser the stitches, the more thread per centimetre.

111
00:09:09,678 --> 00:09:15,914
The looper's swing and its avoiding move are both roughly harmonic and about

112
00:09:15,934 --> 00:09:17,313
90 degrees apart,

113
00:09:17,333 --> 00:09:19,618
so they make a flat ellipse.

114
00:09:20,018 --> 00:09:27,041
Send a sine command to each axis of a SCARA robot or an X–Y table and you get

115
00:09:27,061 --> 00:09:29,236
a circle or ellipse too:

116
00:09:29,256 --> 00:09:35,182
the amplitudes set the shape, the phase difference the direction.

117
00:09:35,582 --> 00:09:40,578
The 401 timing window is only about 7.5 degrees,

118
00:09:40,598 --> 00:09:46,012
just 0.19 milliseconds at 6500 revolutions a minute,

119
00:09:46,032 --> 00:09:52,387
so looper and needle bar must be synchronised by shaft angle.

120
00:09:52,787 --> 00:09:58,067
A robot picking from a moving conveyor does the same:

121
00:09:58,087 --> 00:10:03,167
it sets targets from the conveyor encoder position.

122
00:10:03,567 --> 00:10:05,507
That's an electronic cam.

123
00:10:07,027 --> 00:10:08,965
Chainstitch at home:

124
00:10:08,985 --> 00:10:15,327
rice and flour sacks are often closed with a single-thread chain.

125
00:10:15,727 --> 00:10:18,383
Find the right thread end, pull,

126
00:10:18,403 --> 00:10:23,067
and the whole seam comes undone loop by loop like a zip.

127
00:10:23,467 --> 00:10:30,287
That's the chainstitch's habit of unravelling from an unsecured end,

128
00:10:30,307 --> 00:10:33,607
which here becomes a convenience.

129
00:10:34,007 --> 00:10:36,912
On clothes it's a weakness,

130
00:10:36,932 --> 00:10:44,172
so the end of a 401 seam on jeans has to be secured by other means.

131
00:10:45,692 --> 00:10:46,752
Back to the start.

132
00:10:47,152 --> 00:11:00,446
Each thread needs its ratio times 172 centimetres times 1000 pieces times 1.12.

133
00:11:00,846 --> 00:11:11,446
By the model, each needle thread needs about 4623 metres, 1 cone each;

134
00:11:11,466 --> 00:11:18,425
the looper thread about 23117 metres, 5 cones;

135
00:11:18,445 --> 00:11:24,190
about 32364 metres and 7 cones in all.

136
00:11:24,590 --> 00:11:32,078
The supervisor's total divided by 5000 also gives 7, but only by coincidence:

137
00:11:32,098 --> 00:11:36,954
it says nothing about which thread needs how many.

138
00:11:37,354 --> 00:11:44,060
The looper thread takes 71 percent, and a cone feeds only one thread,

139
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:48,740
so the looper thread must be stocked separately.

140
00:11:50,260 --> 00:11:55,353
Now Virtual lab 10-1, the chainstitch looper and stitch 401.

141
00:11:55,373 --> 00:11:59,444
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

142
00:11:59,464 --> 00:12:03,450
The readings of the five criteria, the verdict,

143
00:12:03,470 --> 00:12:07,199
the settings and the tasks are blurred here.

144
00:12:07,219 --> 00:12:10,523
Read them when you do the lab yourself.

145
00:12:10,923 --> 00:12:15,512
First pause and press Loop-catch moment. The needle has risen,

146
00:12:15,532 --> 00:12:21,014
and in the front and top views you can see the looper point enter the loop

147
00:12:21,034 --> 00:12:22,723
from behind the needle.

148
00:12:23,123 --> 00:12:27,657
Now press Needle at looper height. As the next needle comes down,

149
00:12:27,677 --> 00:12:30,179
the point is already in front of it,

150
00:12:30,199 --> 00:12:35,503
and the needle has to go into the triangle of blade, looper thread and loop.

151
00:12:35,903 --> 00:12:40,933
Task one: press Scramble. All five settings are thrown off.

152
00:12:40,953 --> 00:12:46,753
See which criteria turn red, then bring back the timing, the height,

153
00:12:46,773 --> 00:12:53,515
the avoidance, the clearance and the retraction one by one until all five pass.

154
00:12:53,915 --> 00:13:02,414
Task two: reset, then move only the looper timing, half a degree at a time.

155
00:13:02,434 --> 00:13:05,027
Try retarding it first,

156
00:13:05,427 --> 00:13:11,784
then advancing it. Note which criterion turns red first at each end,

157
00:13:11,804 --> 00:13:18,067
and check the window from the lecture for yourself in the task box.

158
00:13:19,587 --> 00:13:25,186
Virtual lab 10-2, coverstitch and thread use, is on the platform too.

159
00:13:25,206 --> 00:13:29,665
The readings on the consumption chart, the batch table,

160
00:13:29,685 --> 00:13:34,062
the cone count and the tasks are blurred here as well.

161
00:13:34,462 --> 00:13:40,477
Start by switching stitches. 401 has one needle thread and one looper thread.

162
00:13:40,497 --> 00:13:46,042
The face looks like a lockstitch, and the back is a row of chain loops.

163
00:13:46,442 --> 00:13:52,062
605 has three needles and one looper, plus a cover thread,

164
00:13:52,082 --> 00:13:57,507
so both faces are covered. Go through all five stitches,

165
00:13:57,527 --> 00:14:04,022
note each thread's ratio, and compare it with the published figure.

166
00:14:04,422 --> 00:14:09,835
The task: go back to 406 and press the T-shirt hem-and-cuffs preset.

167
00:14:09,855 --> 00:14:14,629
The quantity, the wastage and the cone length are filled in.

168
00:14:14,649 --> 00:14:19,583
Read the batch length and the number of cones for each thread,

169
00:14:19,603 --> 00:14:22,779
and enter them in the task box to check.

170
00:14:23,179 --> 00:14:29,025
Finally, slide the density slowly from 7 stitches per centimetre down to 4.

171
00:14:29,045 --> 00:14:30,903
The stitches spread out.

172
00:14:30,923 --> 00:14:36,535
Think about why sparser stitches use less thread per centimetre of seam.

